BT /F2 14.5 Tf 58.00 762.00 Td (How Search works in a Skip List) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 736.15 Td (The search begins at the top layer and moves horizontally along its linked list. If the target element is found) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 722.75 Td (there, the search stops immediately and returns, this is exactly why the sparse top layer is useful: finding the) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 709.35 Td (element early, at a sparser level, means fewer nodes had to be checked, keeping the time complexity low.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 689.45 Td (Searching in a skip list happens like this:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 669.55 Td (When searching for k:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 72.00 649.65 Td (\225) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 649.65 Td (If k = key, done!) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 72.00 633.75 Td (\225) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 633.75 Td (If k < next key, go down a level.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 72.00 617.85 Td (\225) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 617.85 Td (If k >= next key, go right.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 601.95 Td (This is what gives skip lists their efficiency: Skip lists offer O\(log n\) average complexity for both search and) Tj ET
